<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n22546" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">incontĭnentĭa</span></b> ,
		ae, f.
		incontinens. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>, <i>inability of
				containing</i> or <i>retaining</i> (post-Aug.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">urinae,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					20, 15, 57, § 161</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>, <i>inability of
				restraining one</i>'<i>s desires</i>, <i>greediness</i>, <i>incontinence</i>
			(class.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">multa de incontinentia intemperantiaque
					disseruit,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cic.
					Cael. 11, 25</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
